Two layers invented the deployment tier, and they invented different ones
==========================================================================
Date : 2026-07-29
Mode : unit + tsc + next build (structural checks on both boundaries; no live run
       — the paths are exercised by the existing local-deploy live scenarios)
Gate : make check 1544 -> 1552 (+8)

Decision : DECISIONS D36. This is the one code change that decision carries;
           the rest of D36 is confirming two open items as "not doing".


What was wrong
----------------
The dashboard's natural-language deploy posts exactly this:

    { instruction, model, provider }

No environment. `DeployRequest.environment` defaulted to `"dev"`, so every row
that path wrote recorded a tier the operator never chose — and that is the path
the agent UI uses, i.e. most rows.

Then, further along, `activity-data.ts` mapped a MISSING environment to
`"production"`:

    environment: typeof item.environment === "string" ? item.environment : "production",

One unknown, two confident and contradictory guesses, neither of them the
operator's. Whichever layer answered first won, and nothing anywhere said "not
declared".

The detail page had a third: `head?.environment ?? "dev"`.


The fix — absence survives end to end
---------------------------------------
Same rule `tenant`, `triggered_at` and `resolved_at` already follow in this repo:
store it when known, omit it when not, and let the reader tell the difference.

  boundary  `DeployRequest.environment` / `RollbackRequest.environment`
            -> `str | None = None`. No default: the caller declares a tier or
            does not.
  writer    `_write_row` / `record_rollback` set `deploy_item["environment"]`
            only when a tier was declared. Empty string counts as absence — a
            form submitting "" declared nothing, it did not declare "" as a tier.
  doc       `activity-model.ts`: `environment` moves out of
            `DeploymentRecordCore` into an optional field, because the agent path
            no longer guarantees it.
  reader    the mapper yields `undefined`; five render sites print an em dash;
            the detail page renders nothing rather than "dev".


A guard of mine had the hole it was written to catch
------------------------------------------------------
`test_activity_read_model_matches_writers.py` derives the row shapes by walking
dict literals. Making `environment` conditional means writing it as
`item["environment"] = ...` — a subscript assignment, which that walker did not
see. The field would have read as "produced by nobody" and the phantom check
would have failed on a field that is very much written.

This is the identical hole `scripts/find_unwritten_keys.py` had on its first run,
where it reported the already-fixed `triggered_at` as unproduced. Fixed the same
way, and then one step further: the walker now separates

    always — keys in the dict literal; the row cannot exist without them
    maybe  — keys assigned afterwards, i.e. "stored only when known"

and only `always` counts toward the documented core. Without that split the
guard would have demanded `environment` stay in the core — that is, it would have
argued for the bug.


Falsification (each applied alone; restored green after every one)
-------------------------------------------------------------------
  1. writer drops the conditional store ............ 1 failed / 12 passed
  2. HTTP boundary defaults to "dev" again ......... 1 failed / 12 passed
  3. mapper defaults to "production" again ......... 1 failed / 12 passed
  4. one render site loses its absence branch ...... 1 failed /  6 passed
  5. doc marks environment required again .......... 1 failed / 12 passed
  restored ......................................... 13 passed

Revert 4 PASSED on the first attempt and the guard was innocent: the replacement
never applied. The render sites had been written with a literal `—` escape
sequence rather than an em dash, so the revert's pattern matched nothing. JS
parses `"—"` inside a string literal correctly, so the page was right and
only the test's edit was wrong — but a falsification that silently edits nothing
reports PASS for the same reason a real fix does. Normalised the escapes to the
character, re-ran, and it fails as it should.

Guards (tests/test_deployment_environment_absence.py, 7 tests):
  * behavioural — no declaration means no attribute; a declared tier is kept
    verbatim; empty string is absence; rollback follows the same rule.
  * structural on both boundaries — every `environment` Field in the request
    models must default to None, and the mapper must yield `undefined` rather
    than substituting a tier.
  * derived over the whole dashboard — any `{x.environment}` interpolated into
    JSX with no absence branch fails, so a sixth render site added later is
    caught rather than silently printing nothing.


Verification
--------------
  make check    1552 passed, 1 skipped   (1544 -> 1552, +8)
  npx tsc --noEmit                        clean
  npx next build                          success


Not fixed here (deliberately)
-------------------------------
The structured trigger form still offers `production / staging / dev` while the
registry's envs are `dev` / `prod` per tenant — "staging" exists in neither, and
"production" is not "prod". Reconciling those means deciding whether the tier is
free text or a registry reference, which is the same question D36 answers with
"deployments are not registry-scoped". Left as recorded in the plan rather than
quietly aligned.
